# lambda-call

A lambda bound to a local `val` and invoked directly — the shape real code writes
constantly (`val f = …; f(x)`) and the one **no other corpus row contains**: every
other higher-order workload passes its lambda as an ARGUMENT to a collection method,
which takes a different and much faster runtime path.

Anti-fold: `seed` (opaque at the call site) drives an LCG whose value is both the
lambda's argument and part of the result, so no backend can precompute `workload`.
**The first version of this row omitted that and was DISHONEST** — it made `f` return a
constant, v1 folded the whole loop to nothing (0.029 ns/iteration, a third of a CPU
cycle) and the row read 1734×. See `TASK/v2-perfomance.md` v2-perf-6b.

```scalascript
def workload(seed: Long): Long =
  var s = (seed % 2147483646L) + 1L
  val f = (x: Long) => (x * 48271L) % 2147483647L
  var sum = 0L
  var i = 0
  while i < 100000 do
    s = f(s)
    sum = sum + (s % 7L)
    i = i + 1
  sum
```
